feat: scroll-tinted lore copy; larger mobile hero, tighter lore gap

Three changes.

The hero fills more of a phone screen. --text-mega's middle term goes
from 13vw to 16vw, taking the headline from 51px to 68px at 425px, and
the carousel cells scale from 1.55 to 1.85, taking the first cell from
153px to 183px. Together the headline and strip now reach 61% of the
viewport rather than stopping well short of it. The 3.5rem floor and
12rem ceiling are unchanged, so a 360px screen still wraps to three
lines and a desktop is untouched.

The gap between the lore's opening statement and the narrative beneath
it drops from clamp(3.5rem,9vh,7rem) to clamp(1.75rem,4vh,3rem), and
the paragraph spacing from gap-8 to gap-6. At 425px the distance
between the two blocks falls from about 130px to 41px.

The narrative paragraphs now warm from grey to honey as they pass up
the viewport. The tint is per word rather than per paragraph: a whole
paragraph changing at one threshold reads as a state flip, where
interpolating each word against its own position makes the colour
arrive with the reader. Words are wrapped at runtime rather than in the
markup, so the copy stays plain prose in the source and the effect
follows the real line wrap at any width. Under reduced motion the
passage simply sits at its resting colour with no scrub attached.

Measured mid-scroll: 9 distinct colours across the 169 words, ramping
cleanly from honey at the top of the block to grey at the bottom.
